PROJECT: Build a Complete, Production-Ready Social Media Android App

Act as a senior Android developer, UI/UX designer, backend engineer, database architect, and security engineer. Build a complete, modern, feature-rich social media Android application with a premium user experience, smooth animations, responsive layouts, and fully functional features.

The app must be an original social networking platform with its own identity, design system, and branding. Do not mention or use any existing social media application's name, logo, proprietary assets, or branding anywhere in the project.

IMPORTANT: Build a real, working application, not just a prototype, mockup, static UI, or collection of screens.

1. Technology Stack and Architecture

Use the following technologies where supported by the environment:

Platform: Native Android application.
Language: Kotlin.
UI: Jetpack Compose with Material 3.
Architecture: Clean Architecture with MVVM, repositories, and dependency injection.
Authentication: Firebase Authentication or an equivalent secure authentication provider.
Database: Cloud Firestore or a suitable scalable backend database.
Media storage: Firebase Storage or equivalent cloud object storage.
Push notifications: Firebase Cloud Messaging.
Backend logic: Secure server-side functions for sensitive operations.
Video playback: A suitable Android media playback library.
Image loading: An efficient image-loading library.
Navigation: Type-safe navigation with persistent state where appropriate.

If the environment cannot create a native Android project directly, generate a complete, runnable Android-compatible implementation and provide clear instructions for exporting, building, and running it in Android Studio.

Keep the code modular, maintainable, scalable, testable, and ready for further development.

2. Strict No-Hardcoded-Data Requirement

This is one of the most important requirements.

Do not use hardcoded or fake application data anywhere in the actual app.

Never populate the production interface with:

Fake user accounts or profiles.
Sample posts, stories, reels, or comments.
Fake follower or following counts.
Hardcoded chat conversations or messages.
Fake notifications or activity history.
Fixed usernames, profile photos, or online statuses.
Mock search results or fabricated trending content.
Hardcoded authentication credentials.
Artificial engagement counts, likes, or views.
Placeholder content presented as real user-generated content.

All application data must come from a real backend, authenticated user sessions, or actual user input.

For example:

Display posts from the backend based on the current user's feed and permissions.
Display profile details from the database.
Save likes and comments to the backend.
Load messages from persistent conversation records.
Retrieve notification settings from the current user's account.
Load stories and reels from uploaded media records.
Fetch search results from real indexed user and content records.

Use environment configuration and secure secret management for service credentials. Never expose private server credentials in the Android application.

Create an empty state for every screen that has no data. Examples include "No posts yet", "No messages yet", "No notifications yet", and "No search results".

During development, use an empty database or clearly isolated test fixtures only in automated tests. Never allow test data to appear in the production user experience.

If backend credentials are missing, explain what must be configured and display a useful setup state. Do not silently replace missing backend data with fabricated content.

