18. Mandatory Execution Rules
Start by inspecting the available tools, framework support, and project environment.
Make concrete implementation changes rather than only explaining how to build the app.
Prioritize a working vertical slice before expanding into secondary features.
Never pretend that a backend, database, authentication provider, or external integration has been configured when it has not.
Never fabricate user-generated content or backend results.
Never leave important buttons as nonfunctional placeholders.
Never bypass privacy or security rules to make a feature appear to work.
Do not claim that the project builds, runs, or passes tests without verification.
If a feature cannot be completed in one pass, implement it in clearly defined stages and report the remaining work honestly.
If a required credential or configuration value is missing, identify it and provide exact setup instructions instead of inventing credentials.
Keep the interface consistent with the original design system.
Ensure every feature uses real data, authenticated operations, and persistent storage where required.

Begin by creating the project architecture, implementing the backend configuration, and building the authentication flow. Then continue implementing and testing the app feature by feature. Do not stop after generating a design or a plan.